 ## Overview
-SnugBox is a precise algorithm for computing the axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) of a 3D Gaussian in screen space, replacing the overly conservative tile assignment used in standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3D-GS) [S1]. It solves the problem of overestimating the number of tiles a Gaussian intersects, thereby reducing the number of pixels processed per Gaussian and accelerating rendering [S1][S2, unofficial]. Unlike the standard 3D-GS method which inscribes a circle within a square tile assignment, SnugBox derives a tight bounding box directly from the Gaussian's elliptical extent using its conic equation, providing numerical stability even under edge-on tilts [S1][S3, unofficial]. It serves as a plug-and-play optimization that yields an average inference speed-up of 1.82× without altering the rendered image [S1].
+SnugBox is a precise algorithm for computing Gaussian-to-tile bounding box intersections in 3D Gaussian Splatting, replacing the overly conservative tile assignment of standard 3D-GS to reduce the number of pixels processed per Gaussian [S1][S2, unofficial]. It computes a tight axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) around each Gaussian's elliptical extent in image space, assigning the Gaussian only to tiles that strictly intersect this box [S1][S2, unofficial]. Unlike the standard method which inscribes a circle and uses a square bounding box, SnugBox derives the box directly from the conic equation of the projected Gaussian, providing numerical stability under edge-on tilts [S3, unofficial]. It serves as a lossless optimization that reduces the number of Gaussian-tile pairs, contributing to an average inference speed-up of 1.82× [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:**
-*   3D Gaussian parameters: mean $\mu_i$, scale $s_i$, rotation $r_i$ [S1].
-*   Camera parameters: viewing transform $W$, perspective projection [S1].
-*   Image parameters: tile size $T$, image dimensions [S1].
-*   Precomputed values: 2D covariance matrix $\Sigma_{i_{2D}}$, inverse covariance $\Sigma_{i_{2D}}^{-1}$, and the threshold $t$ derived from opacity $\sigma_i$ [S1].
-
-**Outputs:**
-*   Bounding box edges in pixel coordinates: $x_{min}, x_{max}, y_{min}, y_{max}$ [S1].
-*   Tile indices: $tile_{x_{min}}, tile_{x_{max}}, tile_{y_{min}}, tile_{y_{max}}$ [S1].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
